---
description: C# and Unity coding conventions for Assets/Scripts
globs: **/Assets/Scripts/**/*.cs
alwaysApply: false
---

# Structure
- Keep a class under 400 lines, counting all its `partial` files; past that, split it by concern into separate classes orchestrated by a controller.
- In classes of 100+ lines, separate concerns with `#region` (e.g. `Life Cycle`, `Public Methods`, `Helpers`); do not wrap fields. No `#region` in classes under 100 lines.
- When in doubt, use namespace `Nex`.
- Leave no Rider or Unity warnings or errors in code you edit or review.

# Serialized Fields
- Inspector refs and tunables are `[SerializeField]` camelCase fields, grouped by `[Header("Category")]`.
- Put `[Tooltip(...)]` on the line above `[SerializeField]`; add `[Range(min, max)]` to bounded tunables.
- Map enums to values with `EnumDictionary`, especially when configured in the Inspector.

# Style
- Private fields and methods use camelCase and omit `private`.
- Use `var` for local variable declarations.
- Use braces in control statements, except a one-line guard with a single condition (no `&&`/`||`), a bare `return;`/`continue;`/`break;`, and no trailing comment — e.g. `if (locked) return;`.

# Performance
- No LINQ in runtime code: each call allocates garbage, and GC spikes cause frame hitches. Use `for`/`foreach`. LINQ is fine in Editor-only code; existing starter scripts that use LINQ are not a precedent.
- No `GameObject.Find`: keep a list, iterate child transforms, or pool with `ObjectPooler<T>`.

# Null Handling
- Use `#nullable enable`; initialize required serialized refs with `= null!`.
- Non-nullable references — including `= null!` refs and dependencies passed to `Initialize(...)` — are never null. Do not check them anywhere, and never write `if (x == null) return;`; let a missing reference throw.
- Check only genuinely optional or external data: `T?` values, detection data (poses, nodes), `TryGetValue` lookups, and objects that may be destroyed across an `await`.
- On a `UnityEngine.Object`, do not use `?.`, `??`, or `is null`: they skip Unity's overloaded `==`, so a destroyed object looks alive. Use `obj != null` or `if (obj)`. They are fine on ordinary C# types.

# Lifecycle and Objects
- Wire cross-component dependencies from the parent via `Initialize(...)`, never through `Start`/`Awake` or sibling execution order. `Awake`/`Start` are only for self-contained setup (singleton registration, disabling editor-only objects).
- `Initialize(...)` is synchronous; put awaited setup in a separate async method called right after it.
- Build runtime hierarchies and UI from prefabs with `Instantiate`/`Destroy`, never `new GameObject`.
- For simple inspector behaviour, use `NaughtyAttributes` instead of a custom Editor script. For editor-only code, follow [`editor-script.mdc`](editor-script.mdc).

# Async
- Use `UniTask` instead of coroutines, unless matching existing code in the touched area.
- In a `MonoBehaviour`, pass `destroyCancellationToken`, not `this.GetCancellationTokenOnDestroy()`.

# Compile Errors
- For first-install compile errors about `GlobalOptions`, switch the build profile to `Android`.
